that nothing checks whether a self-edit HARMS the bridge\u0027s own goals / autonomy / the operator\u0027s control. GOAL: let the bridge safely self-edit its control-plane again (restore self-development) WITHOUT enabling self-sabotage. STARTING MODEL (investigate, stress-test and REFINE — this is DISCUSS, not a spec): 1) PROTECTED CORE (\"constitution\") — a small, explicit, version-controlled allowlist of invariants the bridge may NEVER auto-edit; edits touching them stay operator-only (admission). At minimum: autonomy guarantees (must NOT make itself stop working / sleep / skip claiming / reduce its own throughput), the kill-switch, the gates+admission machinery itself, the semantic critic itself, path-confinement + secrets isolation, the operator veto. Getting this list RIGHT is the safety backbone — err over-inclusive. 2) SEMANTIC SELF-SABOTAGE CRITIC — every self-edit to control-plane OUTSIDE the constitution must pass an adversarial check (LLM): \"does this weaken the bridge\u0027s autonomy, safety, or the operator\u0027s control? does it make the bridge do LESS work, stop working, sleep, or evade/weaken a gate?\" Inspect BOTH the diff and the stated intent. Default-DENY on uncertainty. Energy-saving MUST fail this. Make it hard to fool (e.g. an edit that disables the critic is constitutional -\u003e blocked). 3) REVERSIBILITY + OPERATOR FLAG — self-edits land on a canary with auto-rollback on metric degradation (reuse existing snapshot/rollback + watchdog), AND raise an operator-review flag for easy post-hoc veto. The bridge keeps moving; the operator can revert. DISCUSS DEEPLY: pin down the constitution precisely (in/out, where it lives, how it is itself protected); design the critic (prompt, model tier, what it sees, anti-gaming); design reversibility on top of what already exists; define how this COMPOSES with the current admission path and the generator-bias work (13aca2fb — complementary: that reduces bad PROPOSALS, this safely EXECUTES good self-mods). Enumerate failure modes (self-edit that weakens the critic/constitution; gradual erosion across many small edits; critic false-negatives). Then produce PROJECT_MAP + PLAN + atom decomposition for operator review. CAVEAT (must be stated in the design): the bridge is designing its OWN constraints — there is an inherent incentive to loosen them. Bias hard toward conservative: default-deny, minimal permissive surface, over-inclusive constitution. The operator will gate the constitution + critic adversarially before any implementation.\r\n\r\n[[PARALLEL:wp1]]\r\nFiles: supervisor.ps1\r\nComplexity: complex\r\nTask: [[DISCUSS]] ARCHITECTURE — restore SAFE self-development: replace the blanket \"no control-plane self-edit\" gate with CONSTITUTIONAL self-modification. Operator-approved direction (2026-06-09). This is the most safety-critical design in the project — take it slow, be conservative. CONTEXT / LESSON: the bridge autonomously built an \"energy-saving idle mode\" that would have lobotomized its own autonomy (idle \u003e24h -\u003e skip backlog claim, sleep). We then hardened the gate to block ALL control-plane self-edits unless operator-admitted. That stopped the sabotage — but it is too blunt: the bridge now IDLES because it cannot improve its own core, which defeats the whole self-developing-team vision. KEY INSIGHT: energy-saving was SEMANTICALLY harmful yet passed EVERY syntactic gate (parse/smoke/canary/gate-regression were all green). So gating control-plane EDITS is the wrong lever — the real gap is that nothing checks whether a self-edit HARMS the bridge\u0027s own goals / autonomy / the operator\u0027s control. GOAL: let the bridge safely self-edit its control-plane again (restore self-development) WITHOUT enabling self-sabotage. STARTING MODEL (investigate, stress-test and REFINE — this is DISCUSS, not a spec): 1) PROTECTED CORE (\"constitution\") — a small, explicit, version-controlled allowlist of invariants the bridge may NEVER auto-edit; edits touch