that nothing checks whether a self-edit HARMS the bridge\u0027s own goals / autonomy / the operator\u0027s control. GOAL: let the bridge safely self-edit its control-plane again (restore self-development) WITHOUT enabling self-sabotage. STARTING MODEL (investigate, stress-test and REFINE — this is DISCUSS, not a spec): 1) PROTECTED CORE (\"constitution\") — a small, explicit, version-controlled allowlist of invariants the bridge may NEVER auto-edit; edits touching them stay operator-only (admission). At minimum: autonomy guarantees (must NOT make itself stop working / sleep / skip claiming / reduce its own throughput), the kill-switch, the gates+admission machinery itself, the semantic critic itself, path-confinement + secrets isolation, the operator veto. Getting this list RIGHT is the safety backbone — err over-inclusive. 2) SEMANTIC SELF-SABOTAGE CRITIC — every self-edit to control-plane OUTSIDE the constitution must pass an adversarial check (LLM): \"does this weaken the bridge\u0027s autonomy, safety, or the operator\u0027s control? does it make the bridge do LESS work, stop working, sleep, or evade/weaken a gate?\" Inspect BOTH the diff and the stated intent. Default-DENY on uncertainty. Energy-saving MUST fail this. Make it hard to fool (e.g. an edit that disables the critic is constitutional -\u003e blocked). 3) REVERSIBILITY + OPERATOR FLAG — self-edits land on a canary with auto-rollback on metric degradation (reuse existing snapshot/rollback + watchdog), AND raise an operator-review flag for easy post-hoc veto. The bridge keeps moving; the operator can revert. DISCUSS DEEPLY: pin down the constitution precisely (in/out, where it lives, how it is itself protected); design the critic (prompt, model tier, what it sees, anti-gaming); design reversibility on top of what already exists; define how this COMPOSES with the current admission path and the generator-bias work (13aca2fb — complementary: that reduces bad PROPOSALS, this safely EXECUTES good self-mods).